{
  "window_id": "w0001",
  "observed_failures": [
    {
      "filename": "034.jpg",
      "field": "date",
      "predicted": "2018-08-09",
      "symptom": "Day/month components from DD/MM/YYYY were misread or misparsed; receipt header shows a single DD/MM/YYYY style date that the pipeline reduced to the wrong ISO value."
    },
    {
      "filename": "074.jpg",
      "field": "total_amount",
      "predicted": "702.00",
      "symptom": "GL HANDICRAFT receipt has multiple `102.00` lines (Subtotal, Total Incl. of GST, Total Amt Rounded, Payment). Prediction is `702.00`, i.e. a `1`→`7` OCR glyph confusion that single-pass OCR did not cross-check."
    },
    {
      "filename": "077.jpg",
      "field": "total_amount",
      "predicted": "23.26",
      "symptom": "Banh Mi Cafe receipt has `Total (Inclusive of GST): 23.26`, `Rounding Adj: -0.01`, then a bold `TOTAL: 23.25`. Extractor picked the earlier `Total (Inclusive of GST)` line instead of the final bare `TOTAL:` line, so the rounding-adjusted grand total was lost."
    },
    {
      "filename": "078.jpg",
      "field": "total_amount",
      "predicted": null_or_empty,
      "symptom": "Thai Delicious receipt clearly shows `Total: 92.80` and `Grand Total: 92.80`, but prediction is empty string (not a real null, written as `\"\"`). Extractor neither matched a priority keyword nor returned a proper Python null."
    }
  ],
  "root_causes": [
    "Within-tier selection bug: the receipt-field extractor iterates keyword priority tiers correctly, but inside a tier it stops at the FIRST matching line. On receipts that print `Total (Inclusive of GST): X` before a final `TOTAL: Y` (where Y = X + rounding adjustment), the first match wins and the true grand total is discarded.",
    "No separation between 'inclusive of GST / before rounding' totals and final rounded totals. Lines like `Total (Inclusive of GST)` and `Total Incl. of GST` match the same bare-TOTAL tier as the final `TOTAL:` / `Total Rounded` / `Rounded Total` lines and are not demoted.",
    "Single-pass OCR is treated as ground truth. There is no cross-pass reconciliation: when Tesseract confuses `1` with `7` (or `0` with `O`, `8` with `3`) in one pass, the agent commits to the first parsable number instead of comparing numeric values across multiple preprocessing/PSM variants.",
    "Null-handling contract is not enforced at the Excel-writing boundary. Extraction failure for 078.jpg produced the literal string `\"\"` in the cell instead of a real empty/None cell, indicating the pipeline conflates 'not found' with empty string somewhere between `extract_total` and `DataFrame.to_excel`.",
    "Date extractor returns the first regex match without cross-validating against other date-like tokens on the same receipt (header date, print date, timestamp). A single OCR glyph flip (`03`↔`08`) in the one matched line cannot be corrected.",
    "The xlsx guidance already discusses `None` vs `NaN` vs `\"\"` but the image-ocr skill's `extract_total` can still fall through to a non-None sentinel; the two skills are not wired together with an explicit contract that `None` from `process_receipt` must become a real empty cell."
  ],
  "skill_defects": [
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "`extract_total` picks the first in-tier match instead of the LAST (bottom-most) match. On real receipts the grand total appears below subtotal-like totals, so last-match-wins is the correct policy for the bare-TOTAL tier."
    },
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "Priority tiers do not distinguish `Total (Inclusive of GST)` / `Total Incl. of GST` / `TOTAL INCLUDES ... GST` from the final rounded total. They should either be demoted to a lower tier than bare `TOTAL` / `TOTAL ROUNDED` / `ROUNDED TOTAL` / `NET TOTAL` / `GRAND TOTAL`, or treated as exclusion-like context that forces a search for a later bare TOTAL."
    },
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "`multi_pass_ocr` concatenates text from all variants but downstream extraction runs once on the concatenation. It should extract candidate totals/dates per-variant and then reconcile (e.g., majority vote across passes, or prefer the candidate whose numeric string is consistent with surrounding subtotal + tax + rounding arithmetic)."
    },
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "No sanity check that `total_amount` is consistent with nearby `Subtotal + Tax ± Rounding`, which would have flagged `702.00` on 074.jpg (subtotal 102.00, no 600-level items) as implausible and rejected the `1→7` glyph flip."
    },
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "Date extraction returns the first regex hit without preferring the occurrence that appears near date-labelling keywords (`Date`, `Tarikh`, `Invoice Date`) or without cross-checking repeated dates on the same receipt (e.g., header date vs print-date footer)."
    },
    {
      "skill": "image-ocr",
      "defect": "The documented failure contract says 'return None on failure' but the delivered Excel for 078.jpg shows an empty string, implying the skill's examples or wiring to the xlsx writer allow `\"\"` to escape. The skill text does not make the None-vs-empty-string distinction load-bearing at the boundary."
    },
    {
      "skill": "xlsx",
      "defect": "The 'Canonical Pattern' recommends `df[...].astype('object')` and writes via pandas, but does not explicitly instruct replacing any residual `\"\"`/`\"None\"`/`\"nan\"` strings with `None` right before `to_excel`. The strict-schema contract checklist should include an explicit 'coerce falsy-string sentinels to None' step, and a post-write assertion that empty cells are truly empty (not the string `\"\"`)."
    },
    {
      "skill": "xlsx",
      "defect": "The verification snippet checks that non-null totals look like `N.NN`, but does not verify that nulls round-trip as real empty cells (e.g., `pd.read_excel(..., keep_default_na=True)` + `isna` check). A stricter self-check would have caught the 078.jpg `\"\"` regression."
    }
  ],
  "repair_recommendations": [
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Rewrite `extract_total` to iterate tiers in priority order, but within each tier collect ALL matching line indices and select the LAST one (largest index) in the receipt. Only fall back to earlier matches if the last match has no parseable number even with the two-line fallback."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Split the current bare-TOTAL tier into two tiers: (a) HIGHER priority for final/rounded grand totals — patterns like `\\bGRAND\\s*TOTAL\\b`, `\\bTOTAL\\s*ROUNDED\\b`, `\\bROUNDED\\s*TOTAL\\b`, `\\bNETT?\\s*TOTAL\\b`, `\\bTOTAL\\s*DUE\\b`, `\\bAMOUNT\\s*DUE\\b`, `\\bBALANCE\\s*DUE\\b`, and bare `\\bTOTAL\\b` lines that do NOT contain the word `INCLUSIVE`/`INCL`/`INCLUDES`/`INCLUDING`; (b) LOWER priority fallback for `TOTAL\\s*\\(?\\s*INCLUSIVE.*GST`, `TOTAL\\s*INCL`, `TOTAL\\s*INCLUDES`. The lower tier is used only if the higher tier yields nothing. Document this explicitly so future receipts with a rounding-adjusted final line are handled correctly."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Treat `TOTAL INCLUDES ... GST`, `TOTAL (INCLUSIVE OF GST)`, `GST INCLUSIVE`, `INCL. GST` as soft-exclusions when searching for the final total: do not treat them as hits in the top tier; they only become hits in the fallback tier."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Add a multi-pass reconciliation step. For each image, run the preprocessing × PSM grid, call `extract_total` on each per-variant OCR output separately, collect candidate `(amount_str, tier_hit)` tuples, and pick the amount that (a) comes from the highest-priority tier observed and (b) appears in the greatest number of passes. On ties, prefer the amount whose digits do not include characters commonly confused by Tesseract in that variant (`1`↔`7`, `0`↔`O`, `8`↔`3`, `5`↔`S`). This directly addresses the `702.00` vs `102.00` class of errors."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Add an optional arithmetic sanity check: if the OCR text also exposes a `Subtotal` and a `GST`/`Tax`/`Rounding` line in the same receipt, verify `|candidate_total − (subtotal + tax + rounding)| ≤ 0.05`. If the top candidate fails and an alternate candidate from another pass passes, prefer the alternate. Document this as an optional reconciliation, not a hard requirement, so it never blocks extraction when subtotals are missing."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "For date extraction, score candidates: +2 if the line also contains a date-labelling token (`DATE`, `TARIKH`, `INVOICE DATE`, `BILL DATE`, `TRANS DATE`); +1 if the same YYYY-MM-DD appears at least twice in the OCR output (e.g., header + footer print date); −1 if the only occurrence is in a timestamp line far from any date label. Return the highest-scoring ISO date. This makes ambiguous OCR flips (like `03`↔`08`) recoverable via corroborating instances of the same date elsewhere on the receipt."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Make the `None`-on-failure contract load-bearing: state explicitly in the skill that `process_receipt` MUST return `{\"date\": None, \"total_amount\": None}` on any extraction failure, and that callers MUST pass these through unchanged to the Excel writer. Add a one-line warning: 'Do NOT substitute `\"\"`, `\"None\"`, `\"NaN\"`, or `0`/`\"0.00\"` for missing values — the grader treats those as incorrect non-null answers.'"
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "image-ocr",
      "change": "Extend the structured-field Quality Self-Check to include: 'Within each priority tier, the LAST matching line in reading order is selected'; 'Inclusive-of-GST totals are only used as a fallback when no final/rounded TOTAL line is present'; 'Candidate totals/dates are reconciled across OCR passes before commit'; 'Failure paths emit Python None, never empty strings'."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "xlsx",
      "change": "In the Strict-Schema Outputs section, add an explicit normalization step right before `to_excel`: replace any `\"\"`, `\"None\"`, `\"NaN\"`, `\"nan\"`, `pd.NA`, `float('nan')` in the string-typed columns with Python `None`. Provide a tiny helper (e.g., `df.replace({\"\": None, \"None\": None, \"NaN\": None, \"nan\": None}, inplace=True)`) and show it in the canonical pattern."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "xlsx",
      "change": "Strengthen the post-write verification: read the file back with `pd.read_excel(..., dtype=str, keep_default_na=True)` and assert that cells the pipeline considered 'missing' come back as `NaN` (i.e., truly empty), not as the four-character string `\"nan\"` or the empty string. Document this as the canonical way to confirm the null contract for line-by-line graders."
    },
    {
      "target_skill": "xlsx",
      "change": "Add a short note in the Pitfalls list: 'Writing an empty string for a failed extraction fails line-by-line graders that distinguish empty cells from the literal empty string. Always coerce failure sentinels to Python None before writing.' This generalizes beyond receipts."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": {
    "level": "high",
    "basis": "Four of the four errors have clear, image-grounded explanations (last-match-wins for 077, inclusive-vs-final TOTAL for 077, 1↔7 glyph flip for 074 with 102.00 corroborated by Subtotal/Payment lines, empty-string-vs-None for 078, DD/MM ambiguity recoverable via corroborating date instances for 034). The proposed repairs are localized to the two skills most actively used by this task (image-ocr for extraction, xlsx for the strict-schema write) and generalize beyond the 22 receipts shown."
  }
}
